  <h2>Examples</h2>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`-- Returns 2 for valid keys, 1 for NULL_KEY, 0 for invalid keys.
local function isKey(input)
    if input.istruthy then
        return 2
    end
    return input == NULL_KEY
end
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`// Returns 2 for valid keys, 1 for NULL_KEY, 0 for invalid keys.
integer isKey(key input)
{
    if (input) return 2;
    return (input == NULL_KEY);
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local function main()
    -- NULL_KEY itself is evaluated as FALSE only when typecast to key.
    if NULL_KEY.istruthy then  -- Correct.
        ll.OwnerSay("NULL_KEY is TRUE")
    else  -- Never.
        ll.OwnerSay("NULL_KEY is FALSE")
    end

    if (uuid(NULL_KEY)).istruthy then  -- Never.
        ll.OwnerSay("Casted NULL_KEY is TRUE")
    else  -- Correct.
        ll.OwnerSay("Casted NULL_KEY is FALSE")
    end

    -- NULL_KEY won't match keys in lists without typecasting.
    -- The following list contains a null key because avatars don't have creators:
    local details = ll.GetObjectDetails(ll.GetOwner(), {OBJECT_CREATOR})
    local index = ll.ListFindList(details, {NULL_KEY})
    if index >= 0 then  -- Never.
        ll.OwnerSay("NULL_KEY was found in list")
    else  -- Correct.
        ll.OwnerSay("NULL_KEY was NOT found in list")
    end
end

main()
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`default
{
    state_entry()
    {
        // NULL_KEY itself is evaluated as FALSE only when typecast to key.
        if (NULL_KEY) { llOwnerSay("NULL_KEY is TRUE");  } // Correct.
        else          { llOwnerSay("NULL_KEY is FALSE"); } // Never.

        if ((key) NULL_KEY) { llOwnerSay("Casted NULL_KEY is TRUE");  } // Never.
        else                { llOwnerSay("Casted NULL_KEY is FALSE"); } // Correct.

        // NULL_KEY won't match keys in lists without typecasting.
        // The following list contains a null key because avatars don't have creators:
        list details = llGetObjectDetails(llGetOwner(), [OBJECT_CREATOR]);
        integer index = llListFindList(details, [NULL_KEY]);
        if (index >= 0) { llOwnerSay("NULL_KEY was found in list"); } // Never.
        else             { llOwnerSay("NULL_KEY was NOT found in list"); } // Correct.
    }
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h2>Notes</h2>
  <p>Like any <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/LSO" target="_blank">LSO</a> string constants longer then 3 characters and used in multiple places in the code, they should be stored in a global variable. The result will be a considerable memory savings. This does not apply to scripts compiled with <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/Mono" target="_blank">Mono</a>. See <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/LSL_Constants_vs_Globals" target="_blank">LSL Constants vs Globals</a> for more information about this and examples.</p>
  <p>In most situations NULL_KEY isn't needed; an empty string ("") will suffice. To take advantage of this certain practices have to be avoided. In many applications keys are checked against NULL_KEY to determine if they are valid; this is bad practice.</p>
  <p>LSL makes it easy to check if a key is valid. Simply use the key as the parameter for a conditional.</p>
  <p>That is, instead of `if(uuid != NULL_KEY)`, use `if(uuid)`. `if(uuid)` will only return <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/TRUE" target="_blank">TRUE</a> if it is a valid key that is also not a null key.</p>
